# Line-ending policy.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises every text file to LF in the repository AND
# checks it out as LF on every platform. The `eol=lf` half is the load-bearing
# one. Without it the working tree takes the platform's native ending, and two
# failures follow: a hook edited on Windows carries CRLF, so its
# `#!/usr/bin/env sh\r` line resolves to an interpreter name ending in CR and
# fails as "bad interpreter"; and a CRLF checkout leaves the index caching a
# CRLF-sized stat, so once any tool rewrites the file as LF git compares sizes,
# never rechecks the content, and reports the file modified forever.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts are the exception: PowerShell and cmd read their own
# files, and they expect CRLF.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary assets — never normalised, never diffed as text. SVG is XML and stays
# text so its diffs stay readable.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
